WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.470 --> 00:00:06.060
“Imagine a world millions of years in the
future.

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:10.610
Where evolution has written a new chapter
in the story of life.”

00:00:10.610 --> 00:00:16.630
That was the tagline of ‘The Future is Wild,’
a miniseries that aired in the early 2000s

00:00:16.630 --> 00:00:19.710
exploring the speculative lifeforms of the
far future.

00:00:19.710 --> 00:00:22.089
And it was… amazing.

00:00:22.089 --> 00:00:26.869
I think this show left an impact on a lot
of people — myself included.

00:00:26.869 --> 00:00:32.349
I loved this series, and I think my interest
in it helped set me on the path which eventually

00:00:32.349 --> 00:00:34.700
led to making this channel.

00:00:34.700 --> 00:00:39.540
The special effects are, of course, a little
dated by today’s standards, but the world

00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:45.330
is fantastically detailed, and an impressive
team of real scientists came up with the creatures

00:00:45.330 --> 00:00:46.770
that inhabit it.

00:00:46.770 --> 00:00:50.870
And while some of the predictions are pretty
out-there, people forget ‘The Future is

00:00:50.870 --> 00:00:54.960
Wild’ never set out to flawlessly predict
future lifeforms — since that's impossible

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:58.300
— but to get people excited about biology
and earth science.

00:00:58.300 --> 00:01:03.650
And I think these creatures definitely deserve
a nostalgic second look, even if you’ve

00:01:03.650 --> 00:01:05.239
never heard of them before.

00:01:05.239 --> 00:01:10.910
So, for this extra-long entry into the archive,
we’ll be exploring this hypothetical future

00:01:10.910 --> 00:01:15.720
— having fun incorporating contemporary
scientific discoveries that might provide

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:20.940
new insight into these speculative lifeforms,
and just appreciating the richness of the

00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:23.000
world the series created.

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:28.111
So, prepare to jump forwards several million
years — to the first era of ‘The Future

00:01:28.111 --> 00:01:30.770
is Wild.’

00:01:30.770 --> 00:01:37.840
The future is wild is divided into three eras
set 5 million, 100 million, and 200 million

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:39.970
years into the future.

00:01:39.970 --> 00:01:46.590
Our journey begins in the 5-million-year era,
when Earth is locked in an ice age.

00:01:46.590 --> 00:01:51.140
Humans aren’t present in this narrative,
having either left the planet or long since

00:01:51.140 --> 00:01:52.170
died out.

00:01:52.170 --> 00:01:58.360
We’d know this frozen wasteland as Northern
France, but it’s now unrecognizable — buried

00:01:58.360 --> 00:02:02.890
under ice sheets over half a mile, or one
kilometer thick.

00:02:02.890 --> 00:02:08.090
And stalking this icy tundra is the first
lifeform of the future.

00:02:08.090 --> 00:02:14.120
This is a Snowstalker, a saber-toothed predator
searching desperately for a meal.

00:02:14.120 --> 00:02:19.550
The scientists imagine Snowstalkers are the
descendants of wolverines, which have moved

00:02:19.550 --> 00:02:24.560
from predator to apex predator thanks to an
available niche.

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:28.830
Essentially being a bigger and heavier version
of their ancestors — with the addition of

00:02:28.830 --> 00:02:34.580
protruding saber teeth to take down large
prey — the Snowstalker is a grounded speculative

00:02:34.580 --> 00:02:38.650
lifeform, and one of my favorites when I was
younger.

00:02:38.650 --> 00:02:42.600
This snowstalker has journeyed all the way
to the coast in search of food.

00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:46.970
And it might have found some in the form of
Gannetwhales.

00:02:46.970 --> 00:02:52.830
Despite their size, these blubbery beach-dwellers
are actually aquatic birds, and have come

00:02:52.830 --> 00:02:54.880
ashore to lay their eggs.

00:02:54.880 --> 00:03:00.050
Their ancestor is the modern gannet, a bird
that can already dive underwater and swim

00:03:00.050 --> 00:03:01.330
with its wings.

00:03:01.330 --> 00:03:05.980
The concept of a gannet transitioning to a
dolphin-sized animal in just five million

00:03:05.980 --> 00:03:08.550
years is in some ways surprising.

00:03:08.550 --> 00:03:13.220
It might make more sense, for example, to
expect that already aquatic animals like sea

00:03:13.220 --> 00:03:18.370
lions and penguins would be the ones to evolve
into a niche left by whales and their relatives.

00:03:18.370 --> 00:03:23.420
But we now know better than ever from the
fossil record that whales themselves evolved

00:03:23.420 --> 00:03:28.569
from a rodent-like animal into a large, sea-faring
one remarkably quickly.

00:03:28.569 --> 00:03:32.330
So Gannetwhales, though unexpected, aren’t
impossible.

00:03:32.330 --> 00:03:37.390
And while a single Gannetwhale would be a
mighty feast for a Snowstalker, this group

00:03:37.390 --> 00:03:40.430
is just too large to be worth the trouble.

00:03:40.430 --> 00:03:47.160
Further inland, however, another potential
source of food is moving across the plains.

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:52.150
On the horizon, a herd of Shagrats slog their
way through the snow.

00:03:52.150 --> 00:03:57.519
These thick-coated creatures are the descendants
of Alpine Marmets, although the Shagrats are

00:03:57.519 --> 00:04:02.019
over three times larger than their rodent
ancestors.

00:04:02.019 --> 00:04:07.300
And while Shagrats look a bit like musk oxen,
they aren’t much bigger than sheep, so five

00:04:07.300 --> 00:04:12.890
million years is plenty of time for such a
lifeform to emerge upon the tundra.

00:04:12.890 --> 00:04:16.959
Yet Shagrats aren’t big enough to deter
a predator like a Snowstalker.

00:04:16.959 --> 00:04:21.930
All it takes is for one of them to fall behind
the herd, and the weary Snowstaker can at

00:04:21.930 --> 00:04:24.340
last get its meal.

00:04:24.340 --> 00:04:27.210
Yet this meal won’t just feed one mouth.

00:04:27.210 --> 00:04:32.400
This particular Snowstalker is a mother, and
is bringing back food for her two cubs.

00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:38.770
The moment here always stuck out to me — as
it shows us that even in the most competitive

00:04:38.770 --> 00:04:44.060
ecosystems, parental instincts haven’t changed.

00:04:44.060 --> 00:04:47.470
But other ecosystems are even more barren.

00:04:47.470 --> 00:04:52.440
The scientists imagine that to the south,
tectonic and temperature shifts have transformed

00:04:52.440 --> 00:04:56.360
the Mediterranean Sea into vast salt flats.

00:04:56.360 --> 00:04:59.880
Dashing along this harsh expanse is a Cryptile.

00:04:59.880 --> 00:05:04.870
These lizards are some of the only creatures
that can endure this arid desert thanks to

00:05:04.870 --> 00:05:07.930
their clever method of getting moisture.

00:05:07.930 --> 00:05:14.160
Near pools of brine, enormous swarms of Brine
Flies reproduce and take to the air.

00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:18.979
These engorged flies are an excellent source
of food and water for anything that can catch

00:05:18.979 --> 00:05:19.979
them.

00:05:19.979 --> 00:05:22.880
And the Cryptile is an expert fly-trapper.

00:05:22.880 --> 00:05:28.060
The lizard rushes through the huge swarm of
flies, using its neck-frill as a fishing net,

00:05:28.060 --> 00:05:31.630
and licking the trapped flies off the net
as it runs.

00:05:31.630 --> 00:05:36.140
It’s an extreme concept for a lifeform,
but a believable one.

00:05:36.140 --> 00:05:40.930
The Cryptile, like a lot of the best lifeforms
in ‘The Future is Wild,’ takes numerous

00:05:40.930 --> 00:05:46.150
unique traits from modern animals — such
as frilled lizards and salt-lake ground-dragons

00:05:46.150 --> 00:05:51.850
— and combines them into a new, yet still
plausible lifeform.

00:05:51.850 --> 00:05:57.720
Rising out of the salt, the Mediterranean
islands are now mountains of eroded limestone

00:05:57.720 --> 00:05:58.720
rock.

00:05:58.720 --> 00:06:02.090
For Cryptiles, they make for an ideal egg-laying
location.

00:06:02.090 --> 00:06:07.300
Deep fissures in the rock called grykes mark
the island’s surface, and some of these

00:06:07.300 --> 00:06:12.300
miniature canyons are narrow enough that they
are inaccessible to most predators.

00:06:12.300 --> 00:06:16.310
But near the larger grykes, a Cryptile needs
to be on its guard.

00:06:16.310 --> 00:06:21.820
For there is a predator here — a stealthy
Gryken that takes advantage of the natural

00:06:21.820 --> 00:06:23.820
cover the grykes provide.

00:06:23.820 --> 00:06:29.690
The Grykens’ closest ancestor is the pine
marten, a small predator that today navigates

00:06:29.690 --> 00:06:33.110
the branches of trees instead of narrow canyons.

00:06:33.110 --> 00:06:38.979
But five million years is a reasonable stretch
of time for a pine marten to become a deadly

00:06:38.979 --> 00:06:40.190
Gryken.

00:06:40.190 --> 00:06:44.990
Efficient as Grykens are, however, the Cryptile
has the advantage of speed…

00:06:44.990 --> 00:06:49.330
A Cryptile isn’t the preferred prey of the
Grykens, though.

00:06:49.330 --> 00:06:54.380
Instead, the delicate looking pigs known as
Scrofas are their primary food source.

00:06:54.380 --> 00:07:00.030
At a glance, it’s easy to tell the Scrofas
are the descendants of wild boars, but with

00:07:00.030 --> 00:07:02.169
one distinct change.

00:07:02.169 --> 00:07:07.590
Scroffa leg bones have become elongated, with
the animals now walking on the tips of their

00:07:07.590 --> 00:07:08.590
toes.

00:07:08.590 --> 00:07:11.680
It’s an adaptation we’ve documented elsewhere
in nature.

00:07:11.680 --> 00:07:17.220
This hoof design mirrors those of Klipspringer
antelopes, which have similar ballet-shoe

00:07:17.220 --> 00:07:19.990
like hooves to move on rocky outcroppings.

00:07:19.990 --> 00:07:26.830
So, considering the Scrofa’s environment,
their strange hoof structure holds up to scrutiny.

00:07:26.830 --> 00:07:31.810
With pigs this athletic, the Grykens have
their work cut out for them in trying to catch

00:07:31.810 --> 00:07:33.230
one.

00:07:33.230 --> 00:07:38.520
Further south is one of the regions I remember
most distinctly from my childhood.

00:07:38.520 --> 00:07:43.759
Huge grassy plains stretch across the South
American landscape where the Amazon Rainforest

00:07:43.759 --> 00:07:45.780
stood before the ice age.

00:07:45.780 --> 00:07:52.210
And upon this Amazon prairie, a strange troop
of ground-dwelling monkeys called Babookaries

00:07:52.210 --> 00:07:53.830
move through tall grass.

00:07:53.830 --> 00:07:59.699
The Babookaries are fairly intelligent, weaving
strange basket-like traps and placing them

00:07:59.699 --> 00:08:04.220
in shallow lakes to catch small fish, which
are their primary food source.

00:08:04.220 --> 00:08:08.900
While this level of tool use in a primate
might be surprising, we now have even more

00:08:08.900 --> 00:08:15.120
documentation than when this documentary aired
of primates using tools to get their food.

00:08:15.120 --> 00:08:20.110
But the monkey the Babookari are theoretically
evolved from — the red-faced uakari — aren’t

00:08:20.110 --> 00:08:23.789
among those documented using basic tools.

00:08:23.789 --> 00:08:29.250
Capuchin monkeys might be the more expected
ancestor to the plains dwelling Babookari,

00:08:29.250 --> 00:08:33.419
as they’re already transitioning in the
present day to a less-arboreal lifestyle,

00:08:33.419 --> 00:08:37.319
and use stones to break open food.

00:08:37.319 --> 00:08:42.519
But no matter their ancestor, the Babookari’s
existence on the plains is imagined to be

00:08:42.519 --> 00:08:43.839
quite perilous.

00:08:43.839 --> 00:08:49.759
For the troop is always in danger of being
eaten by the mighty Carakillers, enormous

00:08:49.759 --> 00:08:52.379
predatory birds that patrol the grasslands
in groups.

00:08:52.379 --> 00:08:57.389
Like the Babookari, the Carakiller’s ancestor
is a bit surprising, as they’re imagined

00:08:57.389 --> 00:09:01.829
to have evolved from small falcons in just
five million years’ time.

00:09:01.829 --> 00:09:07.430
A larger modern bird like the red-legged seriema
— whose ancestors, the terror birds, are

00:09:07.430 --> 00:09:11.860
the real-world inspiration of the Carakiller
— are another potential starting point for

00:09:11.860 --> 00:09:13.999
these giants.

00:09:13.999 --> 00:09:18.970
But in either case, Carakillers — for all
their strength — can’t do much to a creature

00:09:18.970 --> 00:09:20.620
like a Rattleback.

00:09:20.620 --> 00:09:26.050
These reptile-like armored omnivores are imagined
to have too thick a shell for a Carakiller

00:09:26.050 --> 00:09:27.209
to break through.

00:09:27.209 --> 00:09:32.490
Their ‘armor’ is actually derived from
hair, which has become matted and fused into

00:09:32.490 --> 00:09:34.040
hard scales.

00:09:34.040 --> 00:09:39.220
This is because the Rattleback isn’t a reptile
at all, but a mammal, imagined to have evolved

00:09:39.220 --> 00:09:41.350
from a forest dwelling rodent species.

00:09:41.350 --> 00:09:47.040
They were another of my favorites from this
era — although admittedly a rodent transitioning

00:09:47.040 --> 00:09:51.959
to a reptile-like creature that can move its
scales independently in just five million

00:09:51.959 --> 00:09:56.019
years would be quite the transformation.

00:09:56.019 --> 00:10:01.230
But a relative of the Rattleback that is closer
to its mammalian ancestor dwells in the bleak

00:10:01.230 --> 00:10:03.869
desert of central North America.

00:10:03.869 --> 00:10:09.339
In this ice age desert, the North American
Rattleback braves the intense cold.

00:10:09.339 --> 00:10:14.350
This particular Rattleback is a mother traveling
with her baby, and they are facing the intense

00:10:14.350 --> 00:10:16.600
winds of a sandstorm.

00:10:16.600 --> 00:10:21.930
Luckily, their spines aren’t just adapted
to deter predators, but also to protect themselves

00:10:21.930 --> 00:10:24.209
from the unforgiving environment.

00:10:24.209 --> 00:10:29.629
These creatures are more what one might expect
to emerge in this evolutionary timeframe.

00:10:29.629 --> 00:10:33.750
To find food, the rattlebacks dig underground
for tubers.

00:10:33.750 --> 00:10:39.869
But this mother is unknowingly disturbing
the burrow of a highly unusual subterranean

00:10:39.869 --> 00:10:40.869
lifeform.

00:10:40.869 --> 00:10:46.049
This tiny creature is a Spink, and although
it may not look like it, the Spink is a bird.

00:10:46.049 --> 00:10:52.649
According to the documentary, the Spink evolved
from quails, near-flightless birds that transitioned

00:10:52.649 --> 00:10:54.589
to an underground lifestyle.

00:10:54.589 --> 00:10:59.850
The Spink are another creature that seems
unusually specialized for just five million

00:10:59.850 --> 00:11:05.670
years of transition — although we do have
documentation of other birds, like bee eaters,

00:11:05.670 --> 00:11:07.879
creating burrows in which to lay their eggs.

00:11:07.879 --> 00:11:12.990
But the Spink live almost their entire lives
in their tunnels, and only come out under

00:11:12.990 --> 00:11:14.420
cover of darkness.

00:11:14.420 --> 00:11:21.390
Peculiar as they are, I’ve always found
these tunneling birds strangely adorable…

00:11:21.390 --> 00:11:25.059
Far less adorable are the creatures that swoop
overhead.

00:11:25.059 --> 00:11:31.269
With wingspans of 4 feet, or one meter, these
predators, called Deathgleaners, look like

00:11:31.269 --> 00:11:33.179
giant predatory birds.

00:11:33.179 --> 00:11:38.980
But these are actually bats, which have evolved
to take the spot of apex predators of the

00:11:38.980 --> 00:11:40.600
North American skies.

00:11:40.600 --> 00:11:45.959
It would take a very special extinction event
for bats to outcompete large North American

00:11:45.959 --> 00:11:48.499
birds of prey like eagles and condors.

00:11:48.499 --> 00:11:54.920
But certain modern species of bats like flying
foxes have already reached similar sizes — so

00:11:54.920 --> 00:12:00.019
from a biomechanical perspective, given the
right opportunity, creatures like the Deathgleaners

00:12:00.019 --> 00:12:01.600
could indeed take flight…

00:12:01.600 --> 00:12:03.230
Yet five million years into the future isn’t
the only speculative era ‘The Future is

00:12:03.230 --> 00:12:04.230
Wild’ explores.

00:12:04.230 --> 00:12:09.769
The documentary then jumps to my personal
favorite era 100 million years into the future.

00:12:09.769 --> 00:12:14.449
Beginning in the coastal swamps, this is a
version of Earth where hot and humid climate

00:12:14.449 --> 00:12:18.309
conditions have led to a period of tremendous
biodiversity.

00:12:18.309 --> 00:12:24.930
And no species is more emblematic of this
era of abundance than the Toraton.

00:12:24.930 --> 00:12:30.939
These lumbering giants are huge — described
as being the largest land animals ever, exceeding

00:12:30.939 --> 00:12:33.749
the weight of even the biggest dinosaur.

00:12:33.749 --> 00:12:39.249
So, it’s surprising that the ancestor of
these creatures is a species of tortoise.

00:12:39.249 --> 00:12:44.430
Tortoises might not be the most expected lifeform
to break the size record, especially since

00:12:44.430 --> 00:12:49.209
we now have better research suggesting that
large dinosaurs like sauropods could only

00:12:49.209 --> 00:12:53.689
get so large thanks to their starting advantage
of lightweight bone construction.

00:12:53.689 --> 00:12:59.340
Still, I always loved the concept of giant
tortoises, and other aspects of the Toraton

00:12:59.340 --> 00:13:03.889
— like legs that have shifted to under their
body, and their swampy environment having

00:13:03.889 --> 00:13:10.149
a tremendous amount of available plant matter
-- make a lot of sense considering their size.

00:13:10.149 --> 00:13:14.310
Near the feet of the Toratons dwell a family
of Swampus.

00:13:14.310 --> 00:13:19.700
These excellently named creatures are the
descendants of octopuses, and remarkably,

00:13:19.700 --> 00:13:21.989
they can move on land.

00:13:21.989 --> 00:13:27.429
Strange as this might sound, we now have more
documentation than ever of octopuses moving

00:13:27.429 --> 00:13:32.759
upon the land in a limited capacity, using
their tentacles to haul themselves along.

00:13:32.759 --> 00:13:37.709
The Swampus takes this concept further by
being able to breathe above water to some

00:13:37.709 --> 00:13:41.609
extent, sort of like the strange mudskippers
of today.

00:13:41.609 --> 00:13:46.339
And like the mudskipper, the Swampus spend
part of their time in the water, and aren’t

00:13:46.339 --> 00:13:48.829
true land-dwelling cephalopods.

00:13:48.829 --> 00:13:50.430
Those will come later…

00:13:50.430 --> 00:13:55.980
And every time the Swampus returns to the
water, it has to be on high alert.

00:13:55.980 --> 00:14:01.220
For there’s an apex predator here in the
murky depths that can feed on the Swampus.

00:14:01.220 --> 00:14:07.129
This is a Lurkfish, a descendant of the catfish
that can sense the Swampus’s movement thanks

00:14:07.129 --> 00:14:10.589
to a weak electric field the Lurkfish generates.

00:14:10.589 --> 00:14:14.759
But this terror of the wetland has another
use for electricity.

00:14:14.759 --> 00:14:20.820
Releasing a powerful shock, the Lurkfish stuns
the Swampus, and swallows its meal in a single

00:14:20.820 --> 00:14:21.820
gulp.

00:14:21.820 --> 00:14:27.170
While this menacing lifeform might seem far-fetched,
many modern catfish hunt in a manner that

00:14:27.170 --> 00:14:29.220
is surprisingly similar.

00:14:29.220 --> 00:14:34.509
Certain species of catfish can produce a small
shock thanks to specialized muscles on their

00:14:34.509 --> 00:14:39.399
backs, which the catfish uses to stun its
prey just like a Lurkfish…

00:14:39.399 --> 00:14:45.119
But it’s not just the swamps of this era
that the documentary imagines are much changed.

00:14:45.119 --> 00:14:50.339
Higher global temperatures have caused tremendous
sea level rise, with many lowland regions

00:14:50.339 --> 00:14:51.929
now flooded.

00:14:51.929 --> 00:14:58.410
These vast, shallow seas stretch across the
globe — and are home to remarkable new lifeforms.

00:14:58.410 --> 00:15:01.079
This is an Ocean Phantom.

00:15:01.079 --> 00:15:06.569
Always one of my favorites, this complex looking
creature is a unique type of siphonophore.

00:15:06.569 --> 00:15:12.339
Like modern siphonophores, the Ocean Phantom
is actually composed of numerous different

00:15:12.339 --> 00:15:17.869
lifeforms, some which act as sails to help
it navigate these seas, some which help keep

00:15:17.869 --> 00:15:23.649
the lifeform afloat, and some which trail
below the colony to help catch food.

00:15:23.649 --> 00:15:29.000
The idea of a lifeform using biological sails
might sound a little out there, but some modern

00:15:29.000 --> 00:15:33.609
siphonophores and their relatives have already
evolved something similar.

00:15:33.609 --> 00:15:39.110
So, while the Ocean Phantom is certainly an
unusual lifeform, frankly, so are most modern

00:15:39.110 --> 00:15:41.229
siphonophores…

00:15:41.229 --> 00:15:45.480
Under the water’s surface, small creatures
swim in the shallow current.

00:15:45.480 --> 00:15:50.199
At a glance, these creatures look like fish,
but the scientists imagine they’re actually

00:15:50.199 --> 00:15:54.050
a descendent of sea slugs, or nudibranch.

00:15:54.050 --> 00:15:59.569
These are Reef Gliders, and they play a vital
role in pollinating the red algae that grows

00:15:59.569 --> 00:16:00.569
here.

00:16:00.569 --> 00:16:05.939
Free swimming sea slugs might have once seemed
far-fetched, but the recently-filmed Cephalopyge

00:16:05.939 --> 00:16:11.209
is a nudibranch that has evolved to spend
its entire life swimming in the ocean current.

00:16:11.209 --> 00:16:16.639
So, the concept of a fish-like sea slug such
as the Reef Glider isn’t so unlikely after

00:16:16.639 --> 00:16:17.639
all.

00:16:17.639 --> 00:16:21.059
But the Reef Gliders are in danger here in
the open ocean.

00:16:21.059 --> 00:16:26.059
For the Ocean Phantom is a hunter, and is
quite adept at snatching up Reef Gliders and

00:16:26.059 --> 00:16:28.069
using them to feed the colony.

00:16:28.069 --> 00:16:34.100
But feeding on small Reef Gliders is a dangerous
gambit, for while young Reef Gliders are small,

00:16:34.100 --> 00:16:38.990
adults are large enough to pose a serious
threat to the soft Ocean Phantom.

00:16:38.990 --> 00:16:44.529
Yet in cases where the colony is menaced by
an adult Reef Glider, the Ocean Phantom has

00:16:44.529 --> 00:16:47.040
one last form of protection.

00:16:47.040 --> 00:16:52.479
It has its own personal defense team, in the
form of Spindle Troopers.

00:16:52.479 --> 00:16:57.379
Huge sea spiders that live in the chambers
of the phantom rush to defend it.

00:16:57.379 --> 00:17:01.439
They attack the huge adult Reef Glider, until
it backs off.

00:17:01.439 --> 00:17:06.959
The highly complex symbiosis between the Spindle
Troopers and the Ocean Phantoms have modern

00:17:06.959 --> 00:17:12.140
parallels; as the documentary points out,
body guards aren’t unheard of in nature.

00:17:12.140 --> 00:17:18.250
The trapezoid crab lives among coral, and
protects its home from coral-eating starfish

00:17:18.250 --> 00:17:20.500
with its sharp pinchers.

00:17:20.500 --> 00:17:25.870
The Ocean Phantoms take this a step further,
however, by also providing the Spindle Troopers

00:17:25.870 --> 00:17:31.370
with sustenance produced from red algae that
grows on the Ocean Phantoms back.

00:17:31.370 --> 00:17:36.830
Truly, these siphonophores are almost like
miniature cities floating on the fast shallow

00:17:36.830 --> 00:17:38.830
seas…

00:17:38.830 --> 00:17:42.850
Not all regions of this high-temperature Earth
are underwater.

00:17:42.850 --> 00:17:48.029
A vast rainforest can be found upon a rather
unexpected continent.

00:17:48.029 --> 00:17:53.100
We know this landmass as Antarctica — but
it has changed much from our era, becoming

00:17:53.100 --> 00:17:58.410
warmer and warmer as tectonic plate shifts
caused the landmass to shift upward.

00:17:58.410 --> 00:18:04.929
Now, near the equator, this green Antarctica
has given rise to startling adaptations.

00:18:04.929 --> 00:18:09.240
Birds are the main inhabitants of this region,
as they were some of the few animals able

00:18:09.240 --> 00:18:12.669
to migrate to this island continent over the
millennia.

00:18:12.669 --> 00:18:18.770
But this particular species — the Spitefire
bird — has a defense unlike any we’d recognize.

00:18:18.770 --> 00:18:23.510
When threatened, the Spitefire Bird sprays
a hot, noxious fluid from its nose.

00:18:23.510 --> 00:18:28.900
While we’ve never seen such a defense evolve
in a bird in our time, the bombardier beetle

00:18:28.900 --> 00:18:34.750
can produce a burning, acid-like substance
that can repel almost any predator.

00:18:34.750 --> 00:18:40.910
The Spitefire Bird might just be the most
impressive bird species of the Green Antarctic.

00:18:40.910 --> 00:18:46.870
On the nearby peaks of the Great Plateau,
however, another remarkable species is waiting.

00:18:46.870 --> 00:18:51.990
The mountain range where it dwells was formed
by the tectonic plates of Australia and South

00:18:51.990 --> 00:18:57.090
Eastern Asia colliding — a crash that would
likely result in peaks even higher than the

00:18:57.090 --> 00:18:58.750
Himalayas of today.

00:18:58.750 --> 00:19:03.110
And soaring over these peaks is a Great Blue
Windrunner.

00:19:03.110 --> 00:19:07.980
This giant bird possesses a wingspan of almost
ten feet, or three meters.

00:19:07.980 --> 00:19:14.389
And upon closer inspection, it actually has
four wings, not just two, to help it maintain

00:19:14.389 --> 00:19:15.389
altitude.

00:19:15.389 --> 00:19:19.240
At the time of ‘The Future is Wild’s’
production, the concept of a bird with four

00:19:19.240 --> 00:19:21.679
wings was just speculation.

00:19:21.679 --> 00:19:26.740
After its release, however, a specimen of
the dinosaur genus Microraptor was discovered

00:19:26.740 --> 00:19:29.940
to have had four wings, just like the Windrunner.

00:19:29.940 --> 00:19:34.409
While the Microraptor is of course a lifeform
from Earth’s past instead of its future

00:19:34.409 --> 00:19:40.390
— this discovery shows such a body plan
was, at one point, successful.

00:19:40.390 --> 00:19:45.080
While the Windrunners are majestic, a much
less appealing species lurks in the gloom

00:19:45.080 --> 00:19:47.480
of the Great Plateau’s caves.

00:19:47.480 --> 00:19:54.179
These are Silver Spiders, massive arachnids
that have collected huge piles of grass seeds.

00:19:54.179 --> 00:19:56.759
But Silver Spiders are carnivores.

00:19:56.759 --> 00:19:58.350
The seeds aren’t for them.

00:19:58.350 --> 00:20:01.730
They’re for a small, shy form of rodent.

00:20:01.730 --> 00:20:04.179
The amusingly named Poggle.

00:20:04.179 --> 00:20:08.570
While the hamster-like Poggles themselves
are cute, their role in this ecosystem gave

00:20:08.570 --> 00:20:13.380
me more existential dread during my childhood
than… pretty much anything.

00:20:13.380 --> 00:20:18.820
The documentary imagines the Poggles are,
unsettlingly, the very last of all mammal

00:20:18.820 --> 00:20:20.270
species on earth.

00:20:20.270 --> 00:20:24.870
And while the Silver Spiders allow them to
eat their fill of seeds, this is because the

00:20:24.870 --> 00:20:28.279
Spiders are farming the Poggles.

00:20:28.279 --> 00:20:32.309
In moderation, the spiders attack one Poggle…
and drag it off to be eaten.

00:20:32.309 --> 00:20:37.050
It’s a terrifying fate for the last mammal,
and one which I’m sure caused nightmares

00:20:37.050 --> 00:20:38.289
across the globe.

00:20:38.289 --> 00:20:42.370
Thankfully, it’s probably one of the less
likely hypotheticals.

00:20:42.370 --> 00:20:47.700
Mammals are highly adaptable — having already
survived one mass extinction, and aren’t

00:20:47.700 --> 00:20:53.950
likely to be outcompeted so thoroughly in
all parts of the globe — even in a hundred

00:20:53.950 --> 00:20:54.950
million years.

00:20:54.950 --> 00:21:00.541
But a world without mammals is an interesting
concept to explore as a hypothetical, which

00:21:00.541 --> 00:21:04.580
is part of the idea behind ‘The Future is
Wild’ anyway…

00:21:04.580 --> 00:21:10.600
200 million years into the future, we’ve
reached the final era of ‘The Future is

00:21:10.600 --> 00:21:16.480
Wild,’ which the scientists imagine is home
to the most unrecognizable lifeforms of all.

00:21:16.480 --> 00:21:21.450
This version of Planet Earth now features
a single super continent, and a single global

00:21:21.450 --> 00:21:25.230
ocean — much like the bygone era of Pangea.

00:21:25.230 --> 00:21:30.190
This is indeed where our tectonic plates are
likely heading, and the result would be some

00:21:30.190 --> 00:21:33.350
truly extreme environments.

00:21:33.350 --> 00:21:37.980
On the edge of the coast, what seem like birds
are flapping in the sky.

00:21:37.980 --> 00:21:43.669
But upon closer inspection, these aren’t
birds, but aerial fish, which the documentary

00:21:43.669 --> 00:21:46.280
amusingly calls ‘Flish.’

00:21:46.280 --> 00:21:50.940
The Flish are the icon of the franchise in
a way, and are meant to have evolved from

00:21:50.940 --> 00:21:53.149
the gliding flying fish of today.

00:21:53.149 --> 00:21:59.500
In 200 million years, such a lifeform evolving
more efficient flight and lung-like organs

00:21:59.500 --> 00:22:02.890
isn’t so improbable a hypothetical after
all.

00:22:02.890 --> 00:22:08.450
And the air is one of the safer places to
be, for in the waters of the global ocean,

00:22:08.450 --> 00:22:10.030
sharks are still patrolling.

00:22:10.030 --> 00:22:15.590
This bioluminescent species is a Sharkopath
— another entertaining name.

00:22:15.590 --> 00:22:20.039
Sharks surviving this far into the future
with essentially the same body plan makes

00:22:20.039 --> 00:22:25.399
complete sense, as they’ve been stalking
our oceans for at least 400 million years

00:22:25.399 --> 00:22:27.309
with few changes.

00:22:27.309 --> 00:22:32.639
But the introduction of bioluminescent signals
shows the Sharkopaths have become coordinated

00:22:32.639 --> 00:22:37.450
pack hunters — using their flashes to keep
track of each other during a hunt.

00:22:37.450 --> 00:22:42.620
But while the Sharkopaths are deadly, they
aren’t the largest lifeforms in the Global

00:22:42.620 --> 00:22:43.930
Ocean.

00:22:43.930 --> 00:22:47.890
That distinction goes to the colossal Rainbow
Squid.

00:22:47.890 --> 00:22:52.550
These highly intelligent lifeforms are another
one of my childhood favorites, due to their

00:22:52.550 --> 00:22:56.129
ability to change color to ward off predators.

00:22:56.129 --> 00:23:01.130
Displays like this are also present in the
cephalopods of today thanks to the power of

00:23:01.130 --> 00:23:02.759
Chromatophores.

00:23:02.759 --> 00:23:07.740
These are pigment filled sacs in the skin
that can create remarkably complex colors

00:23:07.740 --> 00:23:11.549
and patterns in response to signals from the
animal’s brain.

00:23:11.549 --> 00:23:16.540
But the Rainbow Squid’s Chromatophores are
imagined to be on another level, able to render

00:23:16.540 --> 00:23:21.629
the creature virtually invisible by matching
the color of the water — or light up the

00:23:21.629 --> 00:23:27.230
Rainbow Squid in a dazzling display to others
of their kind…

00:23:27.230 --> 00:23:30.600
A squid offshoot one day conquering the sea
is one thing.

00:23:30.600 --> 00:23:35.039
But on land, ‘The Future is Wild’ has
one last surprise.

00:23:35.039 --> 00:23:40.490
This perpetually damp rainforest is located
on the northwestern portion of the era’s

00:23:40.490 --> 00:23:41.490
supercontinent.

00:23:41.490 --> 00:23:47.580
And lumbering through the forest is a sixteen-foot,
or five meters tall . . . squid.

00:23:47.580 --> 00:23:52.879
This is a Megasquid — one of the most famous
and debated lifeforms of ‘The Future is

00:23:52.879 --> 00:23:53.879
Wild.’

00:23:53.879 --> 00:23:58.799
Like the Swampus, it’s a cephalopod that
has traded the sea for the land, but unlike

00:23:58.799 --> 00:24:02.129
the swampus, is now fully terrestrial.

00:24:02.129 --> 00:24:07.480
Even in 200 million years, a squid becoming
an elephant sized land-dweller without a rigid

00:24:07.480 --> 00:24:09.769
skeleton seems improbable.

00:24:09.769 --> 00:24:14.740
The documentary claims the squid supports
itself using a network of circular and vertical

00:24:14.740 --> 00:24:16.720
muscles that act as a pseudoskeleton.

00:24:16.720 --> 00:24:22.590
And according to calculations done by McNeill
Alexander, a late British biomechanist who

00:24:22.590 --> 00:24:27.030
worked on the project, the math actually checks
out in favor of the megasquid.

00:24:27.030 --> 00:24:33.210
So, the Megasquid, while extremely speculative,
might not be completely impossible after all.

00:24:33.210 --> 00:24:38.059
Which is a preferable outcome — because
the concept of a giant, forest dwelling squid

00:24:38.059 --> 00:24:39.059
is amazing.

00:24:39.059 --> 00:24:44.240
And in the branches above the Megasquid, the
final creature of ‘The Future is Wild’

00:24:44.240 --> 00:24:45.499
is waiting.

00:24:45.499 --> 00:24:51.139
This is a Squibbon: another type of terrestrial
squid that spends its life swinging from the

00:24:51.139 --> 00:24:53.049
branches of the forest canopy.

00:24:53.049 --> 00:24:58.309
And if that isn’t strange enough, the documentary
imagines that navigating the three-dimensional

00:24:58.309 --> 00:25:01.679
forest has made these creatures highly intelligent.

00:25:01.679 --> 00:25:07.779
Indeed, this 200-million-year journey ends
with a stirring promise of human-like intelligence

00:25:07.779 --> 00:25:09.730
reemerging through the Squibbon.

00:25:09.730 --> 00:25:15.490
It’s a speculative concept that is certainly
unusual, but strangely compelling.

00:25:15.490 --> 00:25:20.289
While humans are gone forever in this future,
the implication that a new civilization of

00:25:20.289 --> 00:25:26.270
intelligent squids will replace us . . . is
perhaps strangely comforting.

00:25:26.270 --> 00:25:33.269
I could explore this world forever, and while
there’s not enough time to cover every single

00:25:33.269 --> 00:25:38.309
lifeform — I hope I’ve shown why ‘The
Future is Wild’ was something really special.

00:25:38.309 --> 00:25:42.749
There wasn’t anything like it on TV at the
time, and there hasn’t really been anything

00:25:42.749 --> 00:25:44.179
quite like it since.

00:25:44.179 --> 00:25:48.950
Hearing top scientists get excited about the
creatures they’ve invented was just so…

00:25:48.950 --> 00:25:49.950
wholesome.

00:25:49.950 --> 00:25:53.220
And I hope they know how much the world they
created meant to people.

00:25:53.220 --> 00:25:58.399
I hear there have been plans to revive the
show that have never quite materialized, and

00:25:58.399 --> 00:25:59.669
I think that’s a shame.

00:25:59.669 --> 00:26:04.730
I feel like a modern remake or sequel with
better effects and even more science would

00:26:04.730 --> 00:26:07.700
really be successful in the age of the internet.

00:26:07.700 --> 00:26:13.080
Maybe one day we’ll get more of The Future
is Wild, but in the meantime, we have the

00:26:13.080 --> 00:26:17.639
incredible original, which has stood the test
of time.

00:26:17.639 --> 00:26:20.140
As always, thanks for watching.

00:26:20.140 --> 00:26:24.840
If you enjoyed this entry, please lend your
support by liking, subscribing, and hitting

00:26:24.840 --> 00:26:29.409
the notification icon to stay up to date on
all things Curious.

00:26:29.409 --> 00:26:41.459
See you in the next video.

